## Solution

We solve Laplace's equation in polar coordinates
$$U_{rr} + \frac{1}{r}U_r + \frac{1}{r^2}U_{\theta\theta} = 0$$
subject to the three conditions at $r=1$:
$$U(1,\theta)=1,\qquad U_r(1,\theta)=1,\qquad U_\theta(1,\theta)=0.$$

### Separation of variables

The general (real) solution of Laplace's equation in polar coordinates is
$$U(r,\theta)=a_0+b_0\ln r+\sum_{n=1}^{\infty}\bigl(a_n r^n+b_n r^{-n}\bigr)\cos(n\theta)+\sum_{n=1}^{\infty}\bigl(c_n r^n+d_n r^{-n}\bigr)\sin(n\theta).$$

### Apply $U(1,\theta)=1$

Setting $r=1$ (note $\ln 1=0$):
$$a_0+\sum_{n=1}^{\infty}(a_n+b_n)\cos(n\theta)+\sum_{n=1}^{\infty}(c_n+d_n)\sin(n\theta)=1.$$

Since the right-hand side is constant, Fourier uniqueness gives
$$a_0=1,\qquad a_n+b_n=0,\qquad c_n+d_n=0\quad(n\ge1). \tag{1}$$

### Apply $U_\theta(1,\theta)=0$

We have
$$U_\theta=\sum_{n=1}^{\infty}-n\bigl(a_n r^n+b_n r^{-n}\bigr)\sin(n\theta)+\sum_{n=1}^{\infty}n\bigl(c_n r^n+d_n r^{-n}\bigr)\cos(n\theta).$$

At $r=1$:
$$\sum_{n=1}^{\infty}-n(a_n+b_n)\sin(n\theta)+\sum_{n=1}^{\infty}n(c_n+d_n)\cos(n\theta)=0,$$
which is automatically satisfied by (1). So this condition is consistent (and redundant given $U(1,\theta)=\text{const}$).

### Apply $U_r(1,\theta)=1$

We have
$$U_r=\frac{b_0}{r}+\sum_{n=1}^{\infty}n\bigl(a_n r^{n-1}-b_n r^{-n-1}\bigr)\cos(n\theta)+\sum_{n=1}^{\infty}n\bigl(c_n r^{n-1}-d_n r^{-n-1}\bigr)\sin(n\theta).$$

At $r=1$:
$$b_0+\sum_{n=1}^{\infty}n(a_n-b_n)\cos(n\theta)+\sum_{n=1}^{\infty}n(c_n-d_n)\sin(n\theta)=1.$$

Again the right-hand side is constant, so Fourier uniqueness gives
$$b_0=1,\qquad a_n-b_n=0,\qquad c_n-d_n=0\quad(n\ge1). \tag{2}$$

### Combine (1) and (2)

From (1): $a_n+b_n=0$; from (2): $a_n-b_n=0$. Adding and subtracting:
$$a_n=0,\qquad b_n=0\quad(n\ge1).$$
Likewise $c_n=0,\ d_n=0$ for all $n\ge1$.

All oscillatory modes vanish, leaving only the radially symmetric part:
$$\boxed{U(r,\theta)=a_0+b_0\ln r=1+\ln r.}$$

### Verification

- $U(1,\theta)=1+\ln 1=1$. ✓
- $U_r=\dfrac{1}{r}\Rightarrow U_r(1,\theta)=1$. ✓
- $U_\theta=0\Rightarrow U_\theta(1,\theta)=0$. ✓
- $U_{rr}=-\dfrac{1}{r^2},\ \dfrac{1}{r}U_r=\dfrac{1}{r^2},\ \dfrac{1}{r^2}U_{\theta\theta}=0$, so $U_{rr}+\dfrac{1}{r}U_r+\dfrac{1}{r^2}U_{\theta\theta}=-\dfrac{1}{r^2}+\dfrac{1}{r^2}+0=0$. ✓

The solution satisfies the PDE and all three initial conditions.

